From a9a449c8ca2b846d164b55b58ffe9934623fedf1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 20:11:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NDS: the slice loop for the JIT inline, timers run when they
 overflow

What melonDS does once per scheduler slice (about 9,000 times a frame at
melonDS's 64 cycles) costs more than the JIT's code on a CPU-bound ROM. All
of it is exact: the same cycles, events and order at any slice.

- FastTimers (SetFastTimers, default off in melonDS): RunFrame runs a CPU's
  timers only once its time reaches TimerNext[cpu], the earliest overflow of
  its counting timers. RunTimer is additive, so the counters catch up then;
  every read of a counter goes through RunTimers first; TimerStart and
  RunTimers recompute TimerNext; the counters are caught up before a
  savestate is written and before sleep mode moves the times. Off, TimerNext
  stays 0 and the timers run every slice as before.
- FastLoop (SetFastLoop, default off; needs FastSched): RunSlicesJIT, RunFrame's
  loop for the JIT with NextTarget, a running CPU's Execute<JIT> block loop
  (no call per slice), a halted CPU nothing interrupts, GPU3D::Run's idle
  case and RunSystem's early-out inline, the scheduler's members through one
  base register. TotalScanlines is checked after an event only (only events
  change it).
- FastSched: RunSystem walks the set bits only (same events, same order, same
  snapshot of the mask) and rebuilds SchedNext on the way; CancelEvent of the
  event SchedNext holds sets it to 0, "to recompute", which RunSystem or
  NextTarget resolves (the divider and sqrt unit cancel and reschedule on
  every parameter write). SchedNextUpdate walks the set bits only.
- ARMJIT::LookUpBlock adds the code base it keeps (JitRXBase) instead of
  calling GetRXBase for every block; Reschedule is inline in ScheduleEvent;
  SchedEvent is 64 bytes.
